R-CV1 R-CV3 R-CV5 R-IX4 R-SO14 R-SO19 R-SO22 R-SO23 R-SO24 R-AP16 R-DL2 (slice 206e2): `tln update --no-probe --dry-run --yes` with the registry of config.toml, on the header of registry/al2-update-window (no roc: pin), with no compiler installed and none on PATH. A probe will run, so the compiler is picked as an id before the index (HeldRoc.picked!, R-SO23).
Registry rows: 2026-10-11 (log 10), 2026-10-12 (log 20), 2026-10-13 (log 40, its sha256 is the one of the fake download). The pick reads the registry list (R-CV1: GET /v1/toolchains?after=0, the pins with the source registry), not the GitHub list; it is the newest allowed compiler, 2026-10-13. The closure query has the held window of the pick (from = to = 2026-10-13, R-AP16).
The first probe downloads the pick (R-SO22, `--yes`): the offer uses the same registry rows (no second GET, R-IX4), `.talon.json` has `sha256_source` `registry` (R-CV5), and the compiler becomes `default-roc` (R-SO24). The domain of the oracle loop is the registry list too (no GitHub list in the command). The new pin is written with the moves; `resolved at log:50`.
